name: hash tables
intro: A hash table finds a value by key in about one step, however many
intro: items it holds. A hash function turns the key into a number; that
intro: number mod the bucket count picks a slot. Two keys in one slot is a
intro: collision, not an error: with chaining each bucket is a linked list.
intro:
intro: FNV-1a is a hash small enough to type from memory:
intro:
intro:     uint32_t h = 2166136261u;
intro:     for (const unsigned char *p = s; *p; p++) {
intro:         h ^= *p;
intro:         h *= 16777619u;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: Unsigned arithmetic wraps by definition, which is what a hash
intro: wants; signed overflow is undefined, so never hash with `int`. Read
intro: the bytes as `unsigned char`: a plain `char` may be signed, and a
intro: byte like 0xC3 would sign-extend and change the hash. It is fast and
intro: fine for tables, and no defence if an attacker chooses the keys.
intro:
intro:     struct entry **bucket = &table[h % nbuckets];
intro:
intro: Lookup walks the chain with `strcmp`. Insert at the front of the
intro: chain; a key already there is updated, not added twice. Delete is
intro: the pointer-to-pointer unlink from the last unit.
intro:
intro: Chains stay short only while the table is not too full. The load
intro: factor is items / buckets. Past about 3/4, allocate twice the
intro: buckets and RELINK every node into the new array. A node's bucket
intro: depends on the bucket count, so recompute it -- keep the old index
intro: and lookups miss keys that are really there. Doubling each time
intro: keeps the average cost per insert small.
intro:
intro: A table has no order: walk the buckets and the output depends on
intro: the hash and the size. To print deterministically, collect the
intro: entries in an array, `qsort` by key, and print that.
---
q: strhash.c -- hash words, pick buckets
spec: Takes a bucket count and one or more words:
spec: `strhash 8 foobar` prints the 32-bit FNV-1a hash of each word as
spec: eight lowercase hex digits, a space, the bucket number (the hash
spec: modulo the bucket count, in decimal), a space, and the word. One
spec: line per word, in argument order.
spec:
spec: FNV-1a: start at 2166136261, and for each BYTE of the word xor the
spec: byte in, then multiply by 16777619, all in uint32_t. Bytes above 127
spec: (any UTF-8 text) must hash as unsigned values. The empty word hashes
spec: to its starting value.
spec:
spec: The bucket count must be a whole number from 1 to 1000000, else
spec: `strhash: bad bucket count` on standard error and exit 1. Fewer than
spec: two arguments prints `usage: strhash BUCKETS WORD...` and exits 2.
file: strhash.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdint.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: #define MAXBUCKETS 1000000
prog: 
prog: /* FNV-1a, 32 bit: xor the byte in, then multiply */
prog: static uint32_t fnv1a(const char *s)
prog: {
prog: 	uint32_t h = 2166136261u;
prog: 	for (const unsigned char *p = (const unsigned char *)s; *p != '\0'; p++) {
prog: 		h ^= *p;
prog: 		h *= 16777619u;
prog: 	}
prog: 	return h;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char *argv[])
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc < 3) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: strhash BUCKETS WORD...\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	char *end;
prog: 	errno = 0;
prog: 	long n = strtol(argv[1], &end, 10);
prog: 	if (errno != 0 || end == argv[1] || *end != '\0' || n < 1 ||
prog: 		n > MAXBUCKETS) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "strhash: bad bucket count\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	for (int i = 2; i < argc; i++) {
prog: 		uint32_t h = fnv1a(argv[i]);
prog: 		printf("%08x %u %s\n", (unsigned)h, (unsigned)(h % (uint32_t)n),
prog: 			   argv[i]);
prog: 	}
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: strhash 8 '' a foobar
case: strhash 1000 hello world; strhash 1 x
case: strhash 16 "$(printf '\303\251')"
case: strhash 0 a 2>&1; echo $?; strhash x a 2>&1; echo $?; strhash 1000001 a 2>&1; echo $?
case: strhash 8; echo $?
hint: h ^= byte then h *= prime, in that order, on a uint32_t; walk the string through a pointer to unsigned char
